\section*{1. Derivation of the Two-Degree-of-Freedom Model}

The glucose control model introduced in lecture is:
\begin{equation}
u
=
\alpha v
+
\beta
\int_{-\infty}^{t}
\lambda e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt',
\end{equation}
alongside:
\begin{equation}
\dot v
=
-\gamma v-\delta u(v+\bar v)+F(t).
\end{equation}

To eliminate the integral, differentiate the first equation with
respect to \(t\):
\begin{align}
\dot u
&=
\alpha\dot v
+
\beta\lambda v
-
\beta\lambda
\int_{-\infty}^{t}
e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt'.
\end{align}

From the original equation,
\begin{equation}
\beta
\int_{-\infty}^{t}
\lambda e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt'
=
u-\alpha v.
\end{equation}

Therefore,
\begin{equation}
\beta\lambda
\int_{-\infty}^{t}
e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt'
=
\lambda(u-\alpha v).
\end{equation}

Substituting this into the differentiated equation gives:
\begin{align}
\dot u
&=
\alpha\dot v
+
\beta\lambda v
-
\lambda(u-\alpha v)\\
&=
-\lambda u
+
\lambda(\alpha+\beta)v
+
\alpha\dot v.
\end{align}

Define:
\begin{equation}
f(u,v)
=
-\gamma v-\delta u(v+\bar v)+F.
\end{equation}

Since,
\begin{equation}
\dot v=f(u,v),
\end{equation}
the model can be written as the two-degree-of-freedom ODE system:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\dot u&=-\lambda u+\lambda(\alpha+\beta)v+\alpha f(u,v),\\
\dot v&=-\gamma v-\delta u(v+\bar v)+F.
\end{aligned}}
\end{equation}

\section*{2. Scaling the Variables}

Following the lecture, introduce the dimensionless variables:
\begin{equation}
v=\bar v v',
\qquad
u=\tilde u\,u',
\qquad
t=\tilde t\,t'.
\end{equation}

For the \(v\)-equation,
\begin{equation}
\frac{\bar v}{\tilde t}\frac{dv'}{dt'}
=
-\gamma\bar v v'
-\delta\tilde u\bar v\,u'(v'+1)
+F.
\end{equation}

Dividing by \(\bar v/\tilde t\) gives:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dv'}{dt'}
=
-(\gamma\tilde t)v'
-(\delta\tilde t\tilde u)u'(v'+1)
+\frac{\tilde t}{\bar v}F.
\end{equation}

Choose the scales so that the coefficients of the first two terms
are equal to one:
\begin{equation}
\gamma\tilde t=1,
\qquad
\delta\tilde t\tilde u=1.
\end{equation}

Hence,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\tilde t=\frac{1}{\gamma},
\qquad
\tilde u=\frac{\gamma}{\delta}.
}
\end{equation}

Define the dimensionless input parameter,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
G=\frac{\tilde t}{\bar v}F
=\frac{F}{\gamma\bar v}.
}
\end{equation}

The \(v\)-equation therefore becomes:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dv'}{dt'}
=
-v'-u'(v'+1)+G.
\end{equation}

Define:
\begin{equation}
f'(u',v')
=
-v'-u'(v'+1)+G.
\end{equation}

For the \(u\)-equation,
\begin{align}
\frac{\tilde u}{\tilde t}\frac{du'}{dt'}
&=
-\lambda\tilde u u'
+
\lambda(\alpha+\beta)\bar v v'
+
\alpha f(u,v).
\end{align}

Using
\begin{equation}
f(u,v)
=
\frac{\bar v}{\tilde t}f'(u',v'),
\end{equation}
gives us:
\begin{align}
\frac{du'}{dt'}
&=
-(\lambda\tilde t)u'
+
\lambda\bar v(\alpha+\beta)
\frac{\tilde t}{\tilde u}v'
+
\alpha\frac{\bar v}{\tilde u}f'(u',v').
\end{align}

Using the scales above, define the dimensionless parameters:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
a=\frac{\lambda}{\gamma},
\qquad
b=\frac{\lambda\bar v\delta}{\gamma^2}(\alpha+\beta),
\qquad
c=\frac{\alpha\bar v\delta}{\gamma}.
}
\end{equation}

Dropping the primes gives the dimensionless system:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\dot u&=-au+bv+cf(u,v),\\
\dot v&=-v-u(v+1)+G,
\end{aligned}}
\end{equation}
where,
\begin{equation}
f(u,v)=-v-u(v+1)+G.
\end{equation}

\section*{3. Equilibrium Solutions}

At equilibrium,
\begin{equation}
\dot u=\dot v=0.
\end{equation}

Since \(f(u,v)=0\) at equilibrium, the first equation gives:
\begin{equation}
-au+bv=0.
\end{equation}

Therefore,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
u^*=\frac{b}{a}v^*.
}
\end{equation}

The second equilibrium condition is:
\begin{equation}
-v-u(v+1)+G=0.
\end{equation}

Substituting,
\begin{equation}
u=\frac{b}{a}v
\end{equation}
gives:
\begin{equation}
-v-\frac{b}{a}v(v+1)+G=0.
\end{equation}

Expanding,
\begin{equation}
-\frac{b}{a}v^2-\frac{b}{a}v-v+G=0.
\end{equation}

Multiplying by \(-a/b\) gives:
\begin{equation}
v^2+v+\frac{a}{b}v-\frac{a}{b}G=0,
\end{equation}
or
\begin{equation}
v^2+
\left(1+\frac{a}{b}\right)v
-\frac{a}{b}G
=0.
\end{equation}

Thus, exactly as in the lecture:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
v^*
=
-\frac{1}{2}
\left(1+\frac{a}{b}\right)
\pm
\frac{1}{2}
\sqrt{
\left(1+\frac{a}{b}\right)^2
+
\frac{4aG}{b}
}.
}
\end{equation}

The corresponding equilibrium values of \(u\) are:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
u^*=\frac{b}{a}v^*.
}
\end{equation}

The two equilibrium solutions coincide when:
\begin{equation}
\left(1+\frac{a}{b}\right)^2
+
\frac{4aG}{b}
=0.
\end{equation}

Solving for \(G\) gives the saddle-node value:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
G_{\mathrm{SN}}
=
-\frac{b}{4a}
\left(1+\frac{a}{b}\right)^2.
}
\end{equation}

Therefore, for \(a>0\) and \(b>0\), there are two real equilibrium
solutions when:
\begin{equation}
G>G_{\mathrm{SN}},
\end{equation}
one equilibrium at:
\begin{equation}
G=G_{\mathrm{SN}},
\end{equation}
and no real equilibrium solutions when:
\begin{equation}
G<G_{\mathrm{SN}}.
\end{equation}

Solving the equilibrium equation for \(G\) gives:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
G
=
v^*
+
\frac{b}{a}v^*(v^*+1).
}
\end{equation}

Thus, the equilibrium branch has the saddle-node shape shown in
lecture. A representative sketch is shown below:

